4. API contract

For example:

POST /v1/events
GET  /v1/events/{event_id}
GET  /v1/events
GET  /health/live
GET  /health/ready
GET  /metrics

A production ingestion request might look like:

POST /v1/events
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: f42ea9ce-dae1-4d2c-a877-cc703d52c71f
{
  "source": "example",
  "type": "user.created",
  "timestamp": "2026-09-22T10:30:00Z",
  "payload": {
    "user_id": "usr_123"
  }
}

Response:

{
  "id": "evt_01K...",
  "status": "processed",
  "created_at": "2026-09-22T10:30:01Z"
}

Errors should use one shape everywhere:

{
  "error": {
    "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR",
    "message": "Request validation failed.",
    "details": [
      {
        "field": "timestamp",
        "reason": "invalid RFC3339 timestamp"
      }
    ],
    "request_id": "req_01K..."
  }
}
5. Strong validation

Don't accept arbitrary JSON without boundaries.

Pydantic should enforce:

maximum string lengths
allowed event types
timestamp format
payload shape
maximum payload size
required fields
forbidden extra fields where appropriate

Example:

class EventCreate(BaseModel):
    model_config = ConfigDict(extra="forbid")

    source: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=100)
    type: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=100)
    timestamp: datetime
    payload: dict[str, Any]

At the HTTP layer also impose a request-body size limit.

6. Idempotency

This is an excellent production-readiness feature for an ingestion service.

Request
   │
   ▼
Idempotency-Key
   │
   ▼
lookup
   │
   ├── exists ─────→ return previous result
   │
   └── absent
        │
        ▼
      process
        │
        ▼
persist key + result atomically

The database should enforce uniqueness.

UNIQUE(idempotency_key)

This prevents duplicate processing caused by client retries.

7. Database design

Example:

events

id                 UUID PK
idempotency_key    VARCHAR UNIQUE
source             VARCHAR NOT NULL
event_type         VARCHAR NOT NULL
payload            JSONB NOT NULL
status             VARCHAR NOT NULL
error_code         VARCHAR NULL
created_at         TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
updated_at         TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL
processed_at       TIMESTAMPTZ NULL

Possible indexes:

(status)
(created_at DESC)
(source, created_at DESC)
(event_type, created_at DESC)

Don't over-index.

Explain why each index exists.

8. Database migrations

Use Alembic.

The deployment process becomes:

new image
   ↓
migration check
   ↓
apply backward-compatible migration
   ↓
start application
   ↓
readiness succeeds
   ↓
traffic enabled

For larger systems, document:

expand
→ migrate/backfill
→ application change
→ contract

instead of destructive migrations.

9. Health model

Use separate probes.

GET /health/live

Means:

process is alive
event loop works

Don't check PostgreSQL here.

Then:

GET /health/ready

Means:

application initialized
database reachable
required dependencies available

DigitalOcean App Platform supports configurable health checks and also liveness checks; failing readiness-style checks can stop traffic while liveness failures can restart components.

That maps beautifully to:

/health/ready
/health/live
10. Graceful shutdown

Make this explicit.

SIGTERM
  ↓
readiness false
  ↓
stop accepting new work
  ↓
complete active requests
  ↓
close DB pool
  ↓
flush telemetry
  ↓
exit

Reviewers notice this.

